The potential vulnerability is related to improper handling of user input, which can lead to a Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability. This vulnerability arises when the web application fails to adequately sanitize or escape user-supplied data before rendering it in the browser. If user inputs, such as merchant names or descriptions, are directly included in the HTML response without proper validation, an attacker could inject malicious scripts that execute in the context of other users' browsers. This could allow the attacker to steal sensitive information, hijack user sessions, or perform actions on behalf of the user, thereby compromising the security and integrity of the web application.

